     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div><pre style=\"font-family:Consolas,&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,monospace;font-size:13.8667px;outline:none;border-width:initial;border-style:none;word-break:break-all;line-height:1.1;color:rgb(0,0,0);white-space:pre-wrap!important\"><br></pre><br>Em segunda-feira, 26 de junho de 2017 15:19:00 UTC+2, Márcio Fernandes  escreveu:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear collegues,<div><br></div><div>I am very new in the world of imperfect detection modeling, but I am really amazed by what someone can do with this powerful tool.</div><div><br></div><div>I am now trying to apply the unmarked package to deal with imperfect detection in my dataset. I have 56 sites in which I made 2-3 replicates. I used the pcount function because I believe that&#39;s my case and then I manged to correct several different taxa for imperfect detection.</div><div><br></div><div>However, some of then presented really low values of detection, so for those I got stuck in the problem of finding the appropriate boundary. Moreover, if i try to use the ranef function to see the corrected abundances I recieve many NaN (not a number) outcomes.</div><div>I see this as an ongoing challenge in the development of more appropriate equations to handle those low values, but I am also would like to question the necessity of correcting something which does seem to be well detected.</div><div><br></div><div>Then because of my problem I also have a doubt: If the detection bias is low does it mean it is ok to not consider a imperfect detection? Is it ok for those well detected taxa if I just use the mean + 95%CI for estimate the abundance in the site?</div></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "CzKVDUibAQAJ",
