   "date": "2017-06-26T18:49:57Z",
   "subject": "Re: When imperfect detection is not so imperfect, is that ok to use just the mean + Confidence Interval?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Sounds like potentially an issue described in this thread (and paper referenced within): <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/unmarked/_WO5U7AeYdQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!topic/unmarked/_WO5U7AeYdQ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792451832066000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0JUHQ4G1d3YtZAXb3CX-h1\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/unmarked/_WO5U7AeYdQ</a> ...in which case, yeah, it may not make sense to present the N-mixture abundance estimates. Perhaps a different mixture distribution provides more reasonable results (see again the referenced the Dennis et al. 2015 paper, and the AHM book Ch. 6 [I think] has a more in depth discussion; also, some of the earlier chapters in the book may provide some useful background, such as the effect of un-modeled heterogeneity on parameter estimation)?<div><br></div><div>Best,</div><div><br></div><div>John</div></div>"
    ]
